R-CM2 R-CM9 R-H7 R-C8 R-AL2 R-SP21 R-FE1 R-PD9 R-OUT2 (slice 010, Q9, Q11, Q16): the closure walk in the command code of `install`, `install --frozen`, `list`, `update` and `verify`, with the fake Fx, 6 runs on 3 projects (all in one dir with a `.git`).
Project p1 has no `talon.toml` (R-SP21: install resolves nothing, fetches the closure of the header and writes nothing). It is an app with the platform, `web` 1.0.0 (its header names core 1.0.0, text 1.0.0) and `text` 2.0.0. The pick has the headers of the 3 direct entries, so the walk starts at level 2. Level 2 is core 1.0.0 and text 1.0.0 (not in the cache: the fake download makes them), level 3 is lex 1.0.0 (named by text 1.0.0, also fetched). The closure has 2 series of acme/text, so each run of p1 prints the R-C8 warning once on stderr (R-CM2: the closure is fetched, R-C8: the warning does not change the exit code).
Run 1 (`install`): the calls of the walk come after the pick. One `Fetch.packages!` for the level 2 URLs only, then one `read_files` of their headers, then the fetch of lex and the read of its header. The packages of level 1 are not read again (the pick holds their headers).
Run 2 (`install --frozen`): no pick, so the walk reads all levels from the cache: one `read_files` for each level and no download (the packages are in the cache now). Run 4 (`update`) and run 5 (`verify`) have the index reads of the pick and then the walk for the levels below the pick.
Run 3 (`list`, project p2): the header has the platform and `mid` 1.0.0 (names text 1.1.0, which names core 1.1.0). Text 1.1.0 is not in the cache (fake download, level 2), core 1.1.0 is. The list has one row for each package of the closure with `direct` or `via <alias>` (the winner of each group, R-H7: only the selected version of a group is read, so lex 1.1.0 and lex 1.2.0 are never fetched). p2 has no R-C8 clash.
Run 6 (`update`, project p3: `text` 1.0.0 moves to 1.1.0, which names core 1.1.0): the walk runs on the header after the planned edit (R-AL2), so it reads core 1.1.0 and not lex 1.0.0 (the closure of the old header). `tree.diff` has the new header of p3 and the 4 new packages in the package cache (core 1.0.0, text 1.0.0, lex 1.0.0, text 1.1.0) and no project file: the walk writes only in the package cache.
